# Request bodies: inline, from a file, as a form, and multipart. Requests go to httpbin.org's /anything,
# which echoes back the body it received ("data", "form" and "files" in the response).

@host = https://httpbin.org
@item = hammer
@fixture = small

### < sends a body file as it is: its {{variables}} are not filled in
POST {{host}}/anything
Content-Type: application/json

< ./bodies/template.json

### <@ fills in the file's {{variables}} first
POST {{host}}/anything
Content-Type: application/json

<@ ./bodies/template.json

### <@latin1 reads the file as ISO-8859-1; Content-Type's charset encodes what is sent
POST {{host}}/anything
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=ISO-8859-1

<@latin1 ./bodies/latin1.txt

### The path can use variables
POST {{host}}/anything
Content-Type: application/json

< ./bodies/{{fixture}}.json

### An inline body is encoded in the charset Content-Type names (UTF-8 if none)
POST {{host}}/anything
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=ISO-8859-1

Crème brûlée for {{item}}, s'il vous plaît.

### A URL-encoded form
POST {{host}}/anything
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

item={{item}}&quantity=3&note=fill%20in

### Multipart: variables in part headers and file paths; file parts are streamed from disk
POST {{host}}/anything
Content-Type: multipart/form-data; boundary=sample-boundary

--sample-boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="item"

{{item}}
--sample-boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="fixture"; filename="{{fixture}}.json"
Content-Type: application/json

< ./bodies/{{fixture}}.json
--sample-boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="template"; filename="template.json"
Content-Type: application/json

<@ ./bodies/template.json
--sample-boundary--
